'code-task: repo=qnfo-workers path=qnfo-code-orchestrator/worker.js
code-anchor: async function stepTask(env, task) {
code-probe: {"paths": ["qnfo-code-orchestrator/worker.js"], "contains": "PATCH-NARROW-1"}

Charter pillar autonomy (roadmap row RM-TP-1-AUTONOMY-ENGINE, qnfo-audit roadmap_implementation id 126). Metric to move: code_task_success_rate_30d (0.52, target at least 0.6). Guards that must not worsen: code_pr_merge_share_7d, code_prs_unmerged_over_120m.
Evidence (read 2026-11-04): PR 6274 (2026-11-02) added the MAX_PATCH_CHARS = 80000 gate before publication, and the patch it measures includes the mirror hunks (worker.js plus deployed-current.worker.js), so the effective cap is about 40000 characters of edit per file. Since then 32 of 85 failed tasks end "patch too large: exceeds verification limit" (24 on 11-02, 3 on 11-03, 6 on 11-04) on qnfo-ops (12), qnfo-research-exec (8), qnfo-cloud-ops (8) and qnfo-fleet-control (5). The gate moved these from late failures to early ones and COST-CUT-CODE-1 feeds the size back, but the retry still asks for the whole goal, so a goal larger than the cap never converges and FAIL-SIGNATURE-STOP-1 ends it after two identical results.
EDIT: on the second PTL_SIG result for one goal, ask for the first independently valid part only (the largest subset of the planned edits that fits under MAX_PATCH_CHARS and leaves the file passing its suites), publish that, and enqueue the remainder as a follow-up task on the same issue with the goal minus the part done. Mark the change PATCH-NARROW-1, cover it in the orchestrator test for PTL_SIG, bump with scripts/worker_release.py.
